Fairness checks
Stratified performance:  
Propose evaluating model performance separately by key groups (e.g., age, gender, race, rural vs. urban) if those fields are available or added later.
Class‑specific metrics:  
Emphasize sensitivity/recall for prediabetes and diabetes, not just overall accuracy.

Threshold review:  
Recommend leadership review of decision thresholds to avoid systematically under‑identifying high‑risk patients.

Governance rules

Model documentation:  
Require a “model card” that documents data source, limitations (self‑report, class imbalance), and intended use.

Equity review:  
Establish an equity review step before deployment, including community or frontline clinician input.

Use constraints:  
Clarify that the model should support—not replace—clinical judgment, especially for marginalized populations.

Data quality improvements

Outlier review:  
Investigate extreme BMI values (e.g., >60) for data entry errors.

Augment with structural data:  
Recommend adding variables such as race/ethnicity, insurance status, and geography (urban/rural) to better understand inequities.

Improve measurement:  
Encourage combining self‑report with clinical data where possible (e.g., lab values, claims).

A model trained on this dataset might learn that most people do not have diabetes and therefore default to predicting “no diabetes” in ambiguous cases. Individuals with moderate BMI, borderline blood pressure, or inconsistent health behaviors could be misclassified as low risk, especially if they resemble the majority class in the data. Without explicit fairness checks, this could disproportionately affect patients from under‑resourced communities whose risk patterns differ from the majority represented in the dataset.
